What would make this a real business case
Honestly: plant figures. Every number above that carries a euro sign is an assumption with a plausible range, and the analysis cannot narrow any of them. What it can supply, and does, is the part a plant cannot get from its own accounts —

how much warning the vibration data actually affords: 53.7 to 79.5 hours;
what that warning costs in false alarms: 0 sustained alarms across 1,419 held-out healthy acquisitions, an upper bound of roughly 1 in 1,000 rather than a measured zero;
how stable it is: Test 3 falls from 57 h to 26 h if the calibrated false-alarm rate is tightened from 1% to 0.5%, while Tests 1 and 2 move by under an hour.

Substituting real values for the four assumptions in the table above turns that into a number a maintenance budget can use. Until then, the sensitivity table is the honest form of the answer.
